2026-10-23 21:41:43.041 W | [FlatTileExtents] /data/pyptouser/qinchuanyu/pto-eager/vllm-ascend-dsv4-pto-0251rc1/tests/pypto_test/hca_tnd_mpmd_20261022/experiments/attention_pair512_v4/operator/deepseek_v4_flash_hca_tnd/attention.py:1024:33: a Vec tile of physical shape [16, 1] and dtype fp32 is addressed as a flat run of bytes, so its column extent must span a whole number of 32-byte units, but 1 x 4 = 4 bytes is not. PTO takes the contiguous axis (row_major makes that the columns) in 32-byte steps, so 8 elements of fp32 is the smallest addressable extent -- a [1, 1], [2, 2] or [4, 4] FP32 tile is equally unallocatable, this is not about the shape being a vector. The *logical* extent is free: allocate 8 on that axis and declare 1 as the tile's valid_shape (`valid_shape=` on pl.load / pl.tile.create + pl.set_validshape), which moves and computes only the real data. A single-element operand is usually better read as a scalar instead -- `pl.read(t, [i, 0])` feeds the scalar form of the op (pl.mul, pl.adds, ...) with no tile at all, which is the spelling to reach for when the tile exists only to carry one value into a broadcast.
